  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1217 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThe upper high is forecast to build westward over the southern \nplains and eastern NM through the weekend, bringing warmer \nconditions further west across the area and allowing a moderate \nheat risk to continue across much of eastern NM. At the same time,\nhigh PWATs will persist across much of central and western NM, \nstill above the 90th percentile and challenging daily records. \nDaily rounds of convection will favor the central mountain chain \nwestward, where locally heavy rainfall may result in localized \nflash flooding. The upper high is forecast to drift back eastward \nfrom early through mid next week while elevated PWATs continue \nover the region thanks to persistent troughing over much of the \nIntermountain West. The latest medium range model solutions \ncontinue to show significant spread with regard to the placement \nand strength of synoptic weather features from mid through late \nnext week, with the GFS advertising a very wet scenario involving \na an approaching upper level trough and deep tap of moisture while\nthe ECMWF advertises a sprawling upper high across much of the \nsouthern US. Needless to say, we have lower forecast confidence \nduring the mid/late week period but it is worth acknowledging the \npotential for an increasingly wet scenario.",
